Xroga Browser Automation

The source includes Nanobrowser v0.3.0, from the Nanobrowser contributors.
Upstream: https://github.com/nanobrowser/nanobrowser
Revision: f1e912d7c9e859eec6638702be920c78a8eff0ea
License: Apache License, Version 2.0. See LICENSE.

Xroga modified the package and build configuration, side panel, model
transport, Workspace connection, and local navigation and write policies.
The default build runs the upstream browser controller and agent in Chrome.
The upstream fixture build remains available for engineering verification.

Adapted ideas and code from other open-source projects (see docs/xroga/oss-capability-ledger.json for pinned commits):
- OpenBrowser (https://github.com/dylansantwani/openbrowser), MIT: batch planner, src/agent/batch-plan.ts (attributed in the file header).
- browser-agent (https://github.com/uiuing/browser-agent), MIT: failure diagnosis and recovery chain, adapted in src/agent/failure-diagnosis.ts from packages/extension/src/engine/orchestrator/healing.ts.
- Agent Skills specification (https://github.com/agentskills/agentskills), Apache-2.0: the SKILL.md frontmatter format implemented in src/agent/skill-md.ts.

coreyhaines31/marketingskills (commit 5b2b400), MIT License, Copyright (c) 2025 Corey Haines: expert points in the bundled Google Ads and Meta Ads skills (src/agent/skills.ts) are adapted from skills/ads and references/platform-setup-checklists.md.

Agentic Awesome Skills (https://github.com/sickn33/agentic-awesome-skills), code under the MIT licence, written content under CC BY 4.0
(see its LICENSE and LICENSE-CONTENT). Xroga ships only a discovery index of it (skill id, category, name and a short description, 2,980 of 3,131 entries; the
pool's own "offensive" entries are left out) generated by scripts/build-aas-index.mjs from a pinned commit, and reads a skill's text from that commit on demand.
Each skill keeps its own upstream licence and attribution, tracked in the repository's docs/sources/sources.md.

idpishield (https://github.com/pinchtab/idpishield, commit 6f889fc), Apache-2.0: the prompt-injection patterns (src/browser-control/core/injection-patterns.ts, generated by scripts/gen-injection-patterns.mjs from patterns/builtin.go) and the scoring in src/browser-control/core/injection-scan.ts are ported from it.
PinchTab (https://github.com/pinchtab/pinchtab), MIT: the HTTP-first site survey in src/agent/site-scan.ts follows its scrape pipeline (written here).
Chrome DevTools MCP (https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp), Apache-2.0, and Playwright MCP (https://github.com/microsoft/playwright, Apache-2.0): the ideas behind diagnostics issues, cookies, storage and weight, read match and styles, and save_page_pdf (written here).

Lighthouse 13.6.0 (https://github.com/GoogleChrome/lighthouse, Apache-2.0), as bundled in Chrome DevTools MCP (https://github.com/ChromeDevTools/chrome-devtools-mcp, Apache-2.0): public/lighthouse/bundle.js is that bundle with only its six Node import specifiers pointed at the browser stand-ins in public/lighthouse/shims (scripts/prepare-lighthouse.mjs); its third-party notices are in public/lighthouse/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.

Browser Harness (https://github.com/browser-use/browser-harness, commit 22ecf85, MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Browser Use): the discovery index of its field-tested site guides and interaction how-tos (src/agent/data/harness-index.json, generated by scripts/build-harness-index.mjs); a guide's text is fetched from that pinned commit when read.

Fabric.js (https://github.com/fabricjs/fabric.js, 7.5.0-beta of the pinned commit, MIT, Copyright (c) 2008-2015 Printio, 2016-present Andrea Bogazzi, Shachar Nen and Fabric.js contributors) and Konva (https://github.com/konvajs/konva, 10.11.0, MIT, Copyright (c) 2011-2013 Eric Rowell, 2014-present Anton Lavrenov): their minified bundles are test fixtures only (fixtures/pages/vendor, with each licence beside it), used to prove the canvas tools on real drawing libraries. They are not part of the shipped extension.

modelcontextprotocol/servers (https://github.com/modelcontextprotocol/servers, Apache-2.0 for new code, MIT for contributions not yet relicensed): the ideas of the fetch server (character-set decoding, paged reads) in backend/src/security/publicBodyText.ts and of the time server (convert one moment between zones) in src/agent/free-times.ts, written fresh.

agentskills/agentskills (https://github.com/agentskills/agentskills, Apache-2.0): the lenient-loading and compaction-protection advice of its client implementation guide in src/agent/skill-md.ts and src/agent/compaction.ts, written fresh.

Tesseract.js 5.1.1 and tesseract.js-core 5.1.1 (https://github.com/naptha/tesseract.js, Apache-2.0) and the "fast" English model of tessdata_fast (https://github.com/tesseract-ocr/tessdata_fast, Apache-2.0): vendored unchanged in public/ocr (licences and notice beside them) for the on-device `ocr` tool, which runs in the offscreen document. Nothing is sent anywhere.

Reference-image search (`find_image`) is an original implementation; the recognition models for ara, urd, hin, spa, fra, deu, por, rus and chi_sim are vendored beside eng in apps/xroga-browser-extension/public/ocr/lang (tessdata_fast, Apache-2.0).

Agentic Awesome Skills (https://github.com/sickn33/agentic-awesome-skills, commit f6f686855a, code MIT, written content CC BY 4.0): since 2026-11-26 the text of 2,774 of its skills travels in public/pool/skills.bin (gzip-compressed, unmodified, generated by scripts/build-aas-bundle.mjs). Each skill keeps the licence and attribution its own upstream gives, listed in the pool's docs/sources/sources.md; skills under AGPL, GPL, source-available or unknown licences are not bundled and are fetched from the pool's pinned commit when read.
